# Plato PDS subset — 8 instances, pinned. The fourth set's scale half.
#
# This header is the shared one for all three plato manifests
# (`plato-pds`, `plato-fome`, `plato-nug`); the other two point at it.
#
#     make plato-pds
#
# **Why a fourth set exists at all.** TODO.md §4. Every verdict in this
# repository before it was taken on 139 models, and four refusals in TODO's
# table name a model population as their reopen condition. D46 says two
# instances are 74% of the standard set's total work. Galabova 2023 §3.7 puts
# HiGHS's own presolve speed-up at 1.10 on netlib against 1.67 on a
# Mittelmann-derived set — same code, same measure, the population alone.
#
# **Source.** Mittelmann's mirror, https://plato.asu.edu/ftp/lptestset/pds/,
# served in netlib's own emps packing but bzip2'd rather than gzip'd. That is
# the whole of the difference and it is why bench/fetch.sh gained `bz2-emps`
# rather than a second pipeline. PDS is the patient-distribution family of
# Carolan, Hill, Kennington, Niemi and Wichmann, "An Empirical Evaluation of
# the KORBX Algorithms for Military Airlift Applications", Operations Research
# 38(2), 1990, 240-248 — the **same paper** the Kennington set comes from, so
# pds-30 … pds-100 continue pds-02 … pds-20 which are already in the tree.
#
# **Licence.** No licence statement exists on that host; its 00README gives
# origins and citations only. The position is the one this repository already
# takes for netlib and Kennington and it is unchanged: the instances are
# fetched at build time, pinned by sha256, and **never enter the repository**
# (.gitignore, PLAN 2.10). Nothing is redistributed.
#
# **Tier: no reference objective.** Netlib has Koch's exact rational optima;
# this set has none, and no value is invented for one. It enters at the tier
# TODO.md §4 specifies — checker verdict, solution digest, determinism and
# work units. The `reference` column is 0.0 and the `source` column is `none`,
# which is what the runner reads to refuse an optimum-scored run over it. A
# manifest that carried a made-up reference would score every correct answer
# against a number nobody measured.
#
#     name  sha256  rows  cols  reference  source  objconst
#
# The sha256 is of the .bz2 exactly as plato serves it.
#
# rows/cols are counted from the expanded MPS by the same rule the netlib
# manifests use: constraint rows only, N rows excluded, distinct COLUMNS
# names. **Cross-checked against Mittelmann's own published size table** at
# plato.asu.edu/ftp/lpopt.html, which agrees on every column count and gives
# one more row for pds-100 (156244 against 156243) and for fome13 (48569
# against 48568) — the objective row, which he counts and JAOS does not. That
# is the same offset the Kennington manifest documents against netlib's
# readme. The three nug instances show **no** offset, which is not explained
# and is recorded here rather than smoothed over.
#
# Size, against where the tree was: ken-18 is 105127 x 154699 and was the
# largest model JAOS had ever read. pds-100 is 156243 x 505360, and its
# expanded MPS is 54.5 MB.
#
pds-30       9cd55024e004590e96f663a619525bcd5a287157d991ccddbe34af9afbcc24ad 49944 154998 0.0 none 0.0
pds-40       9f8a7e44003404c75143fbe9333fd5895a155b29e6bcb46972aaec60d6327cfd 66844 212859 0.0 none 0.0
pds-50       94dd82831ee2730917cf589caaedf508f746add2e9361245f28a9f4360bf0005 83060 270095 0.0 none 0.0
